Link the compression and wininet dependencies privately

StormLib links zlib, bzip2 and wininet with the plain
target_link_libraries() signature, which makes them PUBLIC. They therefore
land in INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES of the exported target:

    set_target_properties(StormLib::storm PROPERTIES
      INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES "ZLIB::ZLIB;BZip2::BZip2;wininet")

but the generated StormLibConfig.cmake never calls find_dependency(ZLIB) or
find_dependency(BZip2), so those imported targets do not exist on the
consumer's side. find_package(StormLib) itself succeeds; the failure only
appears when something actually links the target:

    CMake Error at .../StormLibConfig.cmake:60 (set_target_properties):
      The link interface of target "StormLib::storm" contains:
        ZLIB::ZLIB
      but the target was not found.

None of the three is part of StormLib's API -- neither StormLib.h nor
StormPort.h mentions a zlib or bzip2 type, and wininet is an implementation
detail of the HTTP file streams in FileStream.cpp -- so PRIVATE is what was
meant. For the shared library this package ships, that empties the link
interface of the undefined targets and consumers pick the dependencies up
through the DLL.

Note for a future static build: with a static StormLib, CMake re-exports
PRIVATE dependencies as $<LINK_ONLY:...>, and the consumer would need the
imported targets again. Shipping libstorm.a would additionally require
replacing install(EXPORT StormLibConfig) with a StormLibTargets export
wrapped in a hand-written config file that calls find_dependency().

Not yet sent upstream.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -344,7 +344,7 @@
     endif()
 endif()
 
-target_link_libraries(${LIBRARY_NAME} ${LINK_LIBS})
+target_link_libraries(${LIBRARY_NAME} PRIVATE ${LINK_LIBS})
 add_library(${PROJECT_NAME}::${LIBRARY_NAME} ALIAS ${LIBRARY_NAME}) # Allow users to link StormLib::storm when using add_subdirectory
 target_compile_definitions(${LIBRARY_NAME} INTERFACE __STORMLIB_NO_STATIC_LINK__) #CMake will take care of the linking
 target_include_directories(${LIBRARY_NAME} PUBLIC $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/src>)
